        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          How do you stay cool on a treadmill? Move air across your upper body with a proper fan,
          start in a cool, dry room, wear as little and as light as you comfortably can, keep water
          within reach, and schedule the hard sessions for the coolest part of the day. Those five
          changes deal with nearly every case of a treadmill session that feels hotter and harder
          than it should. The underlying reason is simple: outdoors, running creates its own
          breeze; indoors, you stand still in the same pocket of warm air for the whole session,
          and the room slowly gets warmer around you.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How to Stay Cool on a Treadmill: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Aim a fan at your chest and face from in front of the treadmill, and start
            with the coolest, driest room you can manage.</strong> Moving air is what the treadmill
            takes away and what your body most needs back. Everything else — clothing, water,
            timing — helps at the margins, and none of it replaces airflow.
          </p>
          <p>
            In order of how much difference they typically make:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A real fan, raised and close.</strong> Not the little fan in the console,
            and not a fan on the floor blowing at your shins.</li>
            <li><strong>A cooler room.</strong> Air conditioning, an open window or two, a door
            left open, or a session moved to a cooler time of day.</li>
            <li><strong>Drier air.</strong> In humid weather a dehumidifier or air conditioning
            helps sweat evaporate, which is how sweat cools you at all.</li>
            <li><strong>Less, lighter clothing.</strong> Thin, loose, moisture-wicking fabric.</li>
            <li><strong>Water within reach,</strong> and a willingness to slow down.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are buying kit for this, the fan is the item that matters. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill accessories guide</a>
            names the fan we would buy and puts it in context with the other accessories worth
            owning. If you have come here because your treadmill itself is getting hot, smelling
            or cutting out, that is a different problem: see our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-overheating/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">a treadmill that overheats</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-hotter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Why Running Indoors Feels Hotter</h2>
<p>
            Exercise turns a lot of energy into heat, and your body has to get rid of it to keep
            its core temperature steady. It does that mostly through the skin: warm blood is sent
            towards the surface, heat passes to the surrounding air, and sweat evaporates and
            carries more heat away. All of those depend on the air next to your skin being cooler
            and drier than you are, and being replaced as it warms up.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Outdoors, running replaces that air for you.</strong> At a steady run you move
            through still air at your running speed, which is a continuous breeze across your
            face and chest. It strips away the warm, humid layer next to the skin and helps sweat
            evaporate. Most runners do not notice it as a breeze at all; they notice its absence.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>On a treadmill there is no relative wind.</strong> You stay in one place, so
            the air around you warms and fills with moisture from your sweat, and it is not
            replaced except by whatever circulation the room has. Sweat that does not evaporate
            drips off without cooling you. Our comparison of
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-running-outside/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill and outdoor running</a>
            explains this as the main reason a treadmill run can feel harder than the same pace
            outside, even though the belt itself is mechanically a little easier.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The room warms up as you go.</strong> A person running hard is putting out a
            lot of heat into a closed space, and in a small spare room with the door shut, the
            temperature and humidity rise over the session. The treadmill's motor and electronics
            add some heat too, though far less than you do. The result is that the conditions at
            minute forty are worse than at minute five, which is part of why long indoor sessions
            fade.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The effect shows up in effort and heart rate.</strong> When the body is working
            to shed heat, the same pace tends to feel harder and to bring a higher heart rate.
            Heart rate also drifts upward over long steady sessions, and our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-zones/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill heart rate zones</a>
            notes that this drift is more pronounced indoors because heat dissipates poorly. If
            your easy runs mysteriously stopped being easy in summer, the room is a likelier
            culprit than your fitness.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fans" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Fans: Size, Placement and Aim</h2>
<p>
            A fan is the single cheapest thing that makes a long treadmill session tolerable,
            because it restores the airflow that running outdoors creates for free. But a fan in
            the wrong place does surprisingly little, and most of the advice is about where it
            goes.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why the console fan is not enough</h3>
          <p>
            Many treadmills have small fans built into the console. They are generally weak and
            aimed at a fixed point that may not be where your face and chest are, and they move a
            fraction of the air a standalone fan does. Treat them as a pleasant extra and plan on
            a separate fan anyway.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Height: raise it to your torso</h3>
          <p>
            The deck lifts you eight inches or more off the floor, and your chest and face are
            where most of the useful cooling happens. A fan standing on the floor in front of the
            treadmill blows at your shins and the motor hood. Put it on a stand, shelf, table or
            wall bracket so the airflow reaches your chest, or use a fan with a tilting head and
            aim it up. A tall pedestal fan works if its head reaches high enough.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Position: in front, and slightly to one side</h3>
          <p>
            In front of the treadmill is the natural position, because that is where your face
            is. Slightly off to one side keeps the fan clear of the console, any screen, and the
            area where you step on and off. Keep it out of the path of the belt and well away from
            the rear of the deck, and never let a fan cable run under the treadmill or across the
            deck. If you use two fans, one in front and one at the side gives more coverage than
            two side by side.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Distance and power</h3>
          <p>
            A small desk fan has to be close to do much, and close means in the way. An air
            circulator or a larger fan with a strong throw can sit further back and still reach
            you, which is the reason our accessories guide chose a compact air circulator with a
            tilting head. Vornado says the Vornado 630 moves air up to 70 feet, which is far more
            reach than a treadmill room needs. Whatever you buy, more airflow than you think you
            need, on a low setting most of the time, is better than a fan that is flat out and
            still not enough.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Power: its own outlet</h3>
          <p>
            Plug the fan into a different outlet from the treadmill. Never share a power strip,
            extension cord or surge protector between them. Many treadmill manuals ask for a
            dedicated outlet and prohibit extension cords for the treadmill itself, so the fan
            should never be the reason one appears.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When the air itself is hot</h3>
          <p>
            A fan cools you by moving air across the skin and speeding up sweat evaporation. As a
            general principle, it helps less the hotter and more humid the air it is moving, so in
            a genuinely hot room a fan should be paired with something that cools or dries the air
            — air conditioning, a cooler time of day, or an open window to cooler air outside —
            rather than relied on alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="room" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Room Temperature, Humidity and Ventilation</h2>
<p>
            The fan moves air; the room decides what that air is like. A few changes to the room
            often do as much as the fan.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Know your numbers</h3>
          <p>
            A cheap digital thermometer and hygrometer, placed near the treadmill at about head
            height, tells you the temperature and relative humidity you are actually training in.
            Rooms that feel fine when you sit in them can be warmer than you think, and loft
            rooms, garages and rooms with large south- or west-facing windows can swing a long
            way through the day. Reading it before and after a session also shows you how much the
            session itself warms the room.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What temperature to aim for</h3>
          <p>
            There is no single right figure, and we are not going to invent one. As a rule of
            thumb, most people find a room that feels slightly cool when they walk in at rest is
            about right once they are running, because their own heat output will warm it. If you
            have air conditioning, setting it a few degrees cooler than usual before a session,
            and running it during, is the simplest control you have.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Humidity matters as much as heat</h3>
          <p>
            Sweat only cools you when it evaporates, and it evaporates more slowly into air that
            already holds a lot of moisture. That is why a humid room at a moderate temperature can
            feel worse than a dry room that is warmer. Basements are often humid; so are rooms
            near bathrooms and laundry. Air conditioning dehumidifies as it cools. A standalone
            dehumidifier is the other option, though it gives off some heat of its own, so it
            works best run ahead of the session rather than during.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ventilation</h3>
          <p>
            If the outdoor air is cooler than the room, use it. An open window with the door open
            on the other side of the house gives a cross-draught; a window fan blowing outward
            pulls cooler air in through other openings. Even with nothing open, leaving the door
            of a small room ajar lets the heat and moisture you produce escape rather than
            accumulate. Close blinds on sunny windows during the day if you train in the
            evening.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Garages and basements</h3>
          <p>
            A garage treadmill in summer can be the hardest place in the house to stay cool, and a
            basement is often cool but damp. Both are covered in our
            <a href="/treadmill-in-garage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">garage treadmill guide</a>,
            including what heat and humidity do to the machine. If you are still deciding where
            the treadmill should live, treat airflow and temperature as part of that decision;
            it is far easier than fixing them afterwards.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clothing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. What to Wear on a Hot Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Clothing matters more indoors than outdoors, because there is no breeze to push air
            through the fabric. The aim is to leave as much skin as possible exposed to the
            moving air from the fan, and to wear fabric that does not trap sweat.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Less is more.</strong> Shorts and a singlet, or whatever you are
            comfortable in, will be cooler than a T-shirt and leggings. Every layer you remove is
            more skin in the fan's airflow.</li>
            <li><strong>Thin technical fabric over cotton.</strong> Light synthetic or merino
            fabrics move sweat away from the skin and dry quickly. Cotton holds sweat, gets heavy
            and clings, and once soaked it stops letting air reach the skin.</li>
            <li><strong>Loose rather than tight,</strong> unless tight is what keeps you
            comfortable, so that air can move under the fabric.</li>
            <li><strong>Light colours</strong> make little difference indoors, where there is no
            sun to absorb. Choose by fabric and fit instead.</li>
            <li><strong>A headband or cap without a crown,</strong> or a sweatband on the wrist,
            keeps sweat out of your eyes and off the console. A full cap can trap heat indoors, with
            no sun to keep off.</li>
            <li><strong>Socks and shoes that breathe.</strong> Hot feet are a common treadmill
            complaint. Thin running socks and a breathable upper help.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Keep a small towel on the console or a handrail for your face and hands. Dry hands are
            safer on the rails and the controls, and wiping sweat off your face is a small comfort
            that makes the second half of a session easier to finish.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hydration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Water and Hydration: The Basics</h2>
<p>
            How much to drink, and whether you need anything other than water, depends on your
            body, the session and your health, and it is outside what an equipment site should
            prescribe. What we can say is practical.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Have water within reach.</strong> Most treadmills have bottle holders on
            the console or rails. Use a bottle you can open and drink from with one hand, so you
            are not fumbling with a cap at speed.</li>
            <li><strong>Slow down to drink.</strong> Drinking while running fast on a moving belt
            is a good way to drift off the side or drop the bottle. Ease the speed down, drink,
            then build back up.</li>
            <li><strong>Start the session reasonably hydrated,</strong> rather than trying to
            catch up during it.</li>
            <li><strong>Drink sensibly, not heroically.</strong> Thirst is a reasonable guide for
            most people in ordinary sessions. Drinking very large amounts of plain water during
            long exercise can itself cause problems, which is one reason to ask a doctor or a
            registered dietitian if you are doing long sessions in the heat.</li>
            <li><strong>Electrolyte drinks</strong> are a question for that same professional
            conversation, particularly if you have a medical condition or take medication that
            affects fluid or salt balance.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Keep spills off the treadmill. Water on the belt is slippery, and water in the console
            or the motor compartment is a fault waiting to happen. If a bottle does go over the
            console, unplug the machine and let it dry before using it again.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Session Timing and Structure</h2>
<p>
            When you run, and how you arrange the session, change how hot it gets.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Pick the coolest time</h3>
          <p>
            In a room without air conditioning, early morning is usually the coolest time of day,
            because the house has had the night to shed the day's heat. Late afternoon is often
            the warmest, particularly in rooms that catch the sun. If you can move your hardest
            session of the week to the cool part of the day, do; keep the easy walks for the
            warmer slots.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Put the hard work early in the session</h3>
          <p>
            The room warms and you warm as a session goes on. In a warm room, intervals or a tempo
            block done after the warm-up and before the room heats up are more achievable than the
            same work at the end. Save the easy running for the second half.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Shorten or split</h3>
          <p>
            Two shorter sessions in a day are cooler than one long one, because the room recovers
            in between. On a hot day, cutting a session short is a sensible adjustment, not a
            failure.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Expect the first warm week to be the hardest</h3>
          <p>
            It is commonly described by runners and coaches that the body adapts to training in
            warm conditions over repeated exposure, so the first warm spell of the year tends to
            feel worst. That is a reason to ease into it — shorter, easier sessions at first — not
            a reason to force heat on yourself. Deliberate heat training is a specialist topic for
            a coach and, if you have any health condition, a doctor.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cool down properly</h3>
          <p>
            Do not stop dead at the end of a hard session in a hot room. A few minutes of easy
            walking with the fan still on brings things down gradually, and gives you a moment to
            notice how you actually feel before you step off.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="effort-in-heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Adjusting Effort, Pace and Heart Rate in the Heat</h2>
<p>
            Because heat makes the same pace harder, sticking rigidly to a pace target on a hot
            day can turn an easy run into a hard one. A treadmill makes this worse than the road,
            because the belt holds the speed regardless of how you feel; outdoors you would
            naturally slow a little.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Run by effort, not by speed.</strong> On a warm day, set the speed for the
            effort you intended and accept that the number on the console is lower. The talk
            test — full sentences for easy running, short phrases for moderate, a few words for
            hard — adjusts for heat automatically.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Expect heart rate to read higher.</strong> If you train by heart rate, a warm
            room will push it up at a given pace, and it will drift upward through a long steady
            session. Slow the belt to stay in your intended zone rather than chasing the pace. If
            you use a strap or watch, the heart rate it reports is a training aid, not a safety
            device; any symptom on the list below matters more than any number.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use incline with care.</strong> Swapping speed for incline is a good way to
            reduce impact, but an incline walk at a high grade is still hard work and still makes
            heat. It is not a cooler alternative to running at the same effort.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Compare like with like.</strong> A summer treadmill session will not match a
            winter one at the same pace, and comparing them will make you think you are losing
            fitness. Compare the room temperature in your training log as well as the pace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warning-signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Warning Signs of Heat Illness: When to Stop</h2>
<p>
            Overheating on a treadmill is not just discomfort. Heat-related illness is real, and
            the belt's habit of keeping you going at a set pace makes it easier to push past early
            warnings than it is outdoors. The signs below summarise widely published public-health
            guidance, such as the CDC's; they are not a diagnosis, and if in doubt, stop and get
            help.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Signs that mean stop now and cool down</h3>
          <p>
            Public-health guidance lists the signs of heat exhaustion as including heavy sweating;
            cold, pale and clammy skin; a fast, weak pulse; nausea or vomiting; muscle cramps;
            tiredness or weakness; dizziness; headache; and fainting. If you notice any of these,
            stop the treadmill — use the stop button or pull the safety key rather than jumping
            off a moving belt — then move somewhere cool, loosen clothing, cool your skin with wet
            cloths or a cool shower, and sip water. That guidance advises getting medical help if
            you are vomiting, if symptoms get worse, or if they last longer than an hour.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Signs that mean call 911</h3>
          <p>
            Heat stroke is a medical emergency. The same guidance lists signs including a very
            high body temperature; hot, red, dry or damp skin; a fast, strong pulse; headache;
            dizziness; nausea; confusion; and losing consciousness. If you or someone with you
            shows these, call 911, move the person somewhere cooler, and cool them with cool cloths
            or a cool bath while you wait. Public-health guidance says not to give a person with
            suspected heat stroke anything to drink.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Other reasons to stop</h3>
          <p>
            Chest pain, unusual breathlessness, palpitations or feeling faint are reasons to stop
            the belt and seek advice whatever the temperature. Train with someone else in the
            house on hot days if you can, and make sure they know where the treadmill's safety key
            is and how to stop the belt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setting-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. A Cool Treadmill Set-Up, Step by Step</h2>
<p>
            If you are setting up a treadmill space from scratch, or rearranging one that runs
            hot, this is the order that makes the most difference for the least money.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Choose the room with airflow in mind.</strong> A room with a window you
            can open, a door you can leave open, and ideally air conditioning beats a small closed
            box room, however convenient.</li>
            <li><strong>Leave space in front of the treadmill</strong> for a fan on a stand,
            clear of the console and the step-on area.</li>
            <li><strong>Plan the outlets.</strong> The treadmill on its own wall outlet, the fan
            and anything else on a different one. No strips, no extension cords.</li>
            <li><strong>Buy one good fan before anything else,</strong> and mount it at chest
            height, aimed at your torso and face.</li>
            <li><strong>Add a thermometer and hygrometer</strong> so you know what the room is
            doing.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep a towel and a one-handed water bottle</strong> on the console.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the treadmill's own air intake clear.</strong> The motor draws
            cooling air from around the front of the machine. Do not park the fan stand, a
            laundry basket or anything else against the motor hood.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            The last point protects the machine rather than you, and it is worth keeping
            separate in your mind. A runner overheating and a treadmill overheating have different
            causes and different fixes; the machine's version is about lubrication, belt tension,
            dust in the motor compartment and electrical supply, and it is covered in our
            overheating guide linked at the top of this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. What to Buy to Keep a Treadmill Session Cool</h2>
<p>
            Most of this page costs nothing: opening a window, changing the time of day, wearing
            less. The things worth spending money on are short.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A fan with real reach and an adjustable head,</strong> mounted or stood
            at chest height. This is the one purchase that matters.</li>
            <li><strong>A stand, shelf or wall bracket</strong> to get the fan to the right
            height, if it does not have a tall pedestal.</li>
            <li><strong>A digital thermometer and hygrometer,</strong> which costs little and
            tells you whether the room or the session is the problem.</li>
            <li><strong>A dehumidifier,</strong> if the room is a damp basement or you live
            somewhere humid and have no air conditioning.</li>
            <li><strong>Technical running clothes</strong> in thin, quick-drying fabric.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For the fan itself, our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">pick of treadmill accessories worth buying</a>
            names a compact air circulator chosen for a treadmill room, with notes on placing and
            powering it, alongside the mat, lubricant, heart-rate strap and foot pod. If you train
            by heart rate in a warm room, our guide to the
            <a href="/best-heart-rate-monitors-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best heart rate monitors for treadmill training</a>
            explains why a chest strap gives a steadier reading than a wrist sensor when you are
            sweating heavily.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do I get so hot on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because there is no airflow. Outdoors, running through still air creates a breeze that carries heat away and evaporates sweat. On a treadmill you stay in one place, the air around you warms and fills with moisture, and the room heats up over the session. A fan at chest height and a cooler, drier room fix most of it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should I put a fan for treadmill running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In front of the treadmill, slightly to one side, raised to chest height on a stand, shelf or bracket and aimed at your torso and face. A fan on the floor blows at your shins. Keep it clear of the console, the step-on area and the motor hood, and plug it into a different outlet from the treadmill.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What temperature should a room be for treadmill running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no single correct figure. As a rule of thumb, a room that feels slightly cool when you walk in at rest is usually comfortable once you are running, because your own heat warms it. Humidity matters as much as temperature, so a dry room is easier than a humid one at the same reading.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the built-in fan on a treadmill enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Console fans are generally small, weak and aimed at a fixed point that may not match where your face and chest are. They are a pleasant extra rather than a cooling solution. A separate fan with a strong throw, raised to chest height, moves far more air across the parts of your body that shed the most heat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I drink water while running on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Keep water within reach, start the session reasonably hydrated, and slow the belt down before you drink rather than drinking at speed. Thirst is a reasonable guide for most people in ordinary sessions. How much to drink and whether you need electrolytes depend on your health and the session, so ask a doctor or dietitian for long or hot sessions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When should I stop a treadmill session because of heat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Stop and cool down for dizziness, nausea, headache, cramps, unusual weakness or cold, clammy skin, all listed in public-health guidance as signs of heat exhaustion. Confusion, hot red skin or fainting can mean heat stroke, which is an emergency: call 911. Use the stop button or safety key rather than stepping off a moving belt.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Running Hot on a Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most overheating on a treadmill comes from the room and the set-up rather than the runner. These are the patterns that make it worse.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the fan on the floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The deck lifts you eight inches or more, so a floor fan blows at your shins and the motor hood while your chest and face get nothing. Raise the fan on a stand, shelf or bracket, or tilt it up, so the air reaches your torso. Placement matters more than the size of the fan.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Training in a closed box room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A small room with the door shut traps the heat and moisture you produce, so it gets steadily worse through the session. Open the door, open a window if the air outside is cooler, and use air conditioning or a dehumidifier if you have them. Check the temperature before and after to see the effect.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Holding the summer pace you ran in winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Heat makes the same pace harder and pushes heart rate up, and the belt will not slow down for you. Run by effort or heart rate on warm days, accept a lower number on the console, and log the room temperature alongside the pace so you are not misled into thinking you have lost fitness.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pushing through warning signs because the belt keeps going</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill makes it easy to keep going past dizziness, nausea or a headache, because nothing forces you to stop. Treat those as signals to stop the belt, cool down and drink. Confusion or fainting can mean heat stroke, which needs a 911 call. No session is worth finishing at that cost.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

